  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    ## Overview
    
    The era following the Faction Wars, in which penguin civilization redirected its energies from internecine conflict toward inquiry, invention, and institutional competition. War-weary but unbowed, both colonies turned inward — Aurora Stack codifying the old ice-memory rituals into formal practice, Nightfall Shelf engineering krill-harvesting systems at scales previously unachievable. The great guilds rose to prominence: the Aureate, a merchant guild born from the Still Pools; the Shadow Covens; the Communion of elt-sworn — competing through workshops and laboratories rather than spears.
    
    The age's defining pursuits were the unlocking of fire-core crystals and the refinement of spectralum lenses, both oriented toward a persistent collective ambition: leaving The Berg. Schools of magic formalized. Volcanic veins yielded fire-cores that *sang*. It was, by any honest accounting, an age of wonders.
    
    ## What the Record Obscures
    
    The Flipper Accord, typically presented as the era's stabilizing framework, was renegotiated at least three times in secret before its public form was signed — I have recovered the drafts from administrative ice in the Foundation Depths. The accord held because Aurora's aurora-crystals and the Nightshelf's fire-cores created mutual dependence. Need, however, is not trust, and the official histories elide how frequently that distinction mattered.
    
    The deeper the ice-memory rituals reached, the older the impressions became. Some of those impressions carried weight — presences predating penguin civilization entirely. Not all discoveries invited during this era should have been made. The ice recorded what the chroniclers preferred to frame as triumph, and it recorded what they left out.
    
    ## The Gathering Threat
    
    While penguin-kind refined its cleverness, orca war-pods massed in the outer waters. Their scouts circled closer, testing defenses that penguin-kind, absorbed in its ingenuity, had yet to build.
